# [K57FIX] A CONTINUATION LINE INDENTED LESS THAN THE BLOCK'S FIRST.
#
# THIS FIXTURE USED TO ASSERT A DEFECT (K57, docs/dev/known_issues.md):
# the dedent strip was a BYTE COUNT, so `  line two at two`'s own two-space
# indent, stripped by the four-space depth `    line one at four` set, lost
# its first two content bytes and decoded as `ne two at two` at exit 0 —
# wrong under every revision of the design, and deliberately held open
# through [DD-13b.W23] (w23_impl §7.3, manager ruling r59-R4: two
# independent reds must not share a step).
#
# NOW FIXED: the manager's standing K57FIX ruling is that a continuation
# line indented less than the block's own dedent depth is a REFUSAL by
# name, class value-shape — never silent loss, never silent
# reinterpretation. `tests/rxtsource/` has no known-fail bucket, so this
# fixture is exactly what r59-R4 predicted: the pin (an ACCEPTING check
# asserting the wrong value) went RED the moment the fix landed, and the
# check below is inverted to assert the refusal instead. The file's own
# content is UNCHANGED — same three lines, same shallow second line — so
# the flip is entirely in what the harness now expects of it. HEAD-SCOPED
# (`description` above the first `pattern`), so this fixture is leg-A-only
# by the seam ruling; `prose_dedent_body.rxtin` is the three-leg sibling,
# reaching the same refusal in legs B and C, which carry the identical
# byte-count dedent in their own prose-region code.
description |
    line one at four
  line two at two
    line three at four

pattern a+
m "aaa" 0 3
